<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Daegaya</h1><p class="st-meta">200 CE – 562 CE (approx.) · capital Goryeong</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Gaya kingdom of the 5th and 6th centuries, based at Goryeong, whose court produced the gayageum zither.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Daegaya grew from Banpa-guk, a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byeonhan">Byeonhan</a> statelet in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goryeong">Goryeong</a> valley. Its founding legend names Ijinashi as first king, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suro-of-geumgwan-gaya">Suro of Geumgwan Gaya</a>. It rose to lead the northern Gaya states after Goguryeo's campaign of 400 weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Daegaya's kings drew wealth from iron mines and controlled routes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seomjin-river">Seomjin river</a> and the sea. In 479 King Haji sent an envoy to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-qi">Southern Qi</a> court and received a title. Its royal cemetery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jisan-dong-tombs">Jisan-dong</a> includes tombs with dozens of sacrificed attendants. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-gasil">King Gasil</a> the musician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ureuk">Ureuk</a> composed twelve pieces for the newly devised gayageum.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A marriage alliance with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a> in 522 broke down, and in 562 Silla's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabu">Isabu</a>, with the young hwarang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadaham">Sadaham</a>, conquered Daegaya, ending the last Gaya state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ureuk">Ureuk</a> had already fled to Silla, preserving its music.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 200 CE – 562 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5